// timer module: Erlang-style timers on the unified poll mechanism.
//
// C implementation: src/timer.c — statically registered like net/file/buf,
// because the SCHEDULER itself drives timer firing: both poll loops (the
// worker loop in single-thread mode, the I/O poller thread otherwise)
// clamp their poll() timeout to the earliest timer deadline and deliver
// due messages through the regular send path (mbox_deliver). No timer
// thread, no second event loop — net / timer / io / time.sleep all wake
// from the same poll mechanism.
//
//   send_after(pid, ms, msg)     after ~ms, deliver msg to pid; returns
//                                a timer id (> 0), or 0 on OOM
//   send_interval(pid, ms, msg)  deliver msg every ~ms until cancelled;
//                                returns a timer id (> 0), or 0 on OOM
//   cancel(id)                   1 if a pending timer was cancelled,
//                                0 if the id already fired or was never
//                                valid — cancelling is always safe (same
//                                convention as Erlang's erlang:cancel_timer)
//
// Semantics (documented behaviour, not incidental):
//   - The message is SNAPSHOTTED at registration: later mutations of the
//     caller's copy are not visible to the timer, and the caller may die
//     first — delivery still happens.
//   - A timer whose target has died fires and the message is dropped
//     (same as send to a dead pid: silent, no error).
//   - cancel(id) after the timer has fired returns 0 and the message is
//     NOT recalled — once delivered it is delivered. A cancel that races
//     a due fire may return 0 while one delivery is still in flight;
//     cancel only guarantees no FUTURE fire.
//   - Interval timers re-arm from the fire time: a slow tick delays the
//     next fire rather than firing a burst of catch-up deliveries.
//   - Delivery is best-effort real-time: expect scheduler-tick latency
//     (a few ms), so use wide lower bounds, not wall-clock precision.

external fn timer.send_after(pid : pid, ms : int, msg) -> int
external fn timer.send_interval(pid : pid, ms : int, msg) -> int
external fn timer.cancel(id : int) -> int
